## 3. Codebase Analysis & Strategic Guidance

The following analysis is based on my direct review of the current codebase. Use these notes and tips to guide your implementation.

### Relevant Existing Code
*   **File:** `modules/core-platform/src/main/java/villagecompute/storefront/api/rest/StorefrontResource.java`
    *   **Summary:** JAX-RS resource that serves Qute templates for `/` and `/category/{slug}`; it wires in `CatalogService`, builds hero/collection data, maps products/categories to display DTOs, handles pagination & filters, and injects localization + tenant theme tokens before rendering `Templates.index()` or `Templates.catalog()`.
    *   **Recommendation:** Extend this resource (and the nested `@CheckedTemplate` class) as you add new storefront pages so every route provides `msg`, `themeTokens`, `rootCategories`, SEO metadata, and tenant identity; reuse helper methods (`mapProductsToDisplayData`, filter builders, theme loader) instead of duplicating logic for future product, cart, or account pages.
*   **File:** `modules/core-platform/src/main/resources/templates/base.html`
    *   **Summary:** Global Qute layout responsible for `<head>` metadata, CSS/PrimeUI includes, theme token injection via `{#include storefront/_theme.css /}`, the skip link, and the shared header/footer components that rely on `tenantName`, `tenantLogo`, `rootCategories`, `cartItemCount`, and the translation map `msg`.
    *   **Recommendation:** Ensure every new storefront template wraps itself in `{#include base.html}` and provides `head`, `content`, and optional `scripts` sections; populate the data keys mentioned above from your resource so the header/footer remain localized and accessible.
*   **File:** `modules/core-platform/src/main/resources/templates/StorefrontResource/index.html`
    *   **Summary:** Implements the home experience—hero include, category rail, featured product grid, and optional collections—showing how to compose component partials and reference localized copy through `msg.*` keys.
    *   **Recommendation:** Use this file as the blueprint for other page layouts: keep sections wrapped in `{#if}` checks tied to data presence, prefer component includes (e.g., `components/product-card.html`), and make sure CTA URLs map to the routes defined in the architecture doc.
*   **File:** `modules/core-platform/src/main/resources/templates/StorefrontResource/catalog.html`
    *   **Summary:** Represents the category grid + filters view with breadcrumbs, filter sidebar (`components/filters.html`), product grid, pagination, and progressive-enhancement scripts (sorting, filter toggles, pill removal) attached via `data-*` attributes.
    *   **Recommendation:** Mirror this structure for cart/checkout/account templates by factoring reusable UI (filter pills, breadcrumbs, CTA buttons) into shared components when possible and keeping the inline scripts unobtrusive so Percy screenshots stay stable and Lighthouse budgets remain achievable.

### Implementation Tips & Notes
*   **Tip:** Component partials already exist under `modules/core-platform/src/main/resources/templates/components/` (hero, product-card, filters, header, footer); build any new pieces (loyalty badge, cart line items, checkout steps) in that folder and include them from your pages to keep styling consistent with Tailwind + PrimeUI conventions.
*   **Tip:** `StorefrontResource` currently hardcodes `locale = "en"` but loads strings via `localizationService.loadMessages(locale)`; as you introduce ES copy, wire Accept-Language parsing and update `messages.properties` / `messages_es.properties` so the `{msg.*}` lookups used throughout the templates stay valid.
*   **Tip:** Theme data is injected via `themeTokens` and rendered through `_theme.css`; when you add new CSS variables or rely on tokens inside inline styles, extend `buildThemeTokens` instead of hardcoding colors.
*   **Tip:** Catalog data helpers such as `buildBreadcrumbs`, `buildFilterData`, and `mapProductsToDisplayData` already provide DTOs with fields like `price`, `hasVariants`, `inStock`; reuse/extend them so product, cart, and checkout views share formatting and remain ready for future integrations (inventory, reviews, loyalty) without refactors.
*   **Warning:** The current controllers stub out values (prices always `$0.00`, hero image null, etc.); any new UI must degrade gracefully when optional data is missing and should gate experimental sections behind feature-flag checks once the FeatureToggle service is wired into the resource.
